course: biology
lesson: 239
title: Bees: the waggle dance, the hive and honey
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A beehive on a summer day is a city of about 50,000 bees, and every bee in it has a job.
explain: When a worker finds good flowers, she comes home and dances a figure of eight on the comb; the dance tells her sisters which way to fly and how far.
explain: Bees turn flower nectar into honey to eat in winter, store it in six-sided wax cells and guard the door against robbers.
explain: Winnie the Pooh only wants the honey, but a real bear raiding a bee nest also eats the juicy grubs, which is the meal that really fills it up.
fact: The waggle dance shows the direction to the flowers by its angle from straight up, compared with the sun, so a waggle straight up means «fly towards the sun», and the distance by how long the waggle lasts.
fact: Karl von Frisch won a Nobel Prize in 1973 for working out the bee dance.
fact: A hive can have about 50,000 bees: one queen, tens of thousands of female workers and some hundreds of males called drones.
fact: Workers change jobs as they age: cleaning, feeding young, building wax, guarding and finally fetching food; a summer worker lives about five or six weeks.
fact: Bees make honey from nectar by adding enzymes and fanning it with their wings until most of the water is gone, and it feeds them in winter.
fact: Real bears and honey badgers raid bee nests and eat the protein-rich grubs as well as the honey.
fact: A honeybee's sting has barbs that stick in thick mammal skin, so the bee dies, and the left-behind sting keeps pumping venom, so scraping it out quickly helps.
fact: A swarm is the old queen with about half the workers looking for a new home; scouts dance for places and the swarm picks the one with most supporters; a swarm hanging on a branch is usually calm, and a beekeeper can collect it.
fact: The old story that science proves bumblebees cannot fly came from a wrong simple calculation; bees beat their wings very fast and make swirls of air that lift them.
fact: Honeycomb cells are hexagons, which hold the most honey for the least wax, and wax is costly because bees eat a lot of honey to make it.
fact: Many kinds of wild bee are in danger from fewer flowers, disease and some pesticides, and bees pollinate crops like apples, strawberries and almonds.
fact: Guard bees at the door smell each incoming bee to check it carries the hive's own smell, though a stranger bee bringing nectar is sometimes let in, as if she paid a toll.
fact: The queen lays the eggs but gives no orders; she lays up to about 2,000 eggs a day in summer, and any female larva fed only royal jelly can become a queen.
exercise: What does the waggle dance tell other bees? | Which way and how far to fly to the flowers | Which of all the bees in the hive is the queen | When it will rain | That a bear is near the hive and coming for the honey
exercise: How does the dance show the distance? | By how long the waggle lasts | By how loud the bee buzzes while it dances | By the colour of the dancing bee | By how many bees stop to watch
exercise: Who worked out the bee dance and won a Nobel Prize? | Karl von Frisch | Charles Robert Darwin | Sir Isaac Newton | Alexander Fleming
exercise: Who are most of the bees in a hive? | Female workers | Males called drones | Only the queens | Only the baby bees
exercise: What are male bees called? | Drones | Workers | Guard bees | Scout bees
exercise: How long does a summer worker bee live? | About five or six weeks | About five years | About one day | About fifty years, like a person
exercise: What is a worker bee's last job in life? | Fetching food from flowers | Laying eggs to help the old queen | Cleaning cells | Building new wax comb
exercise: How do bees turn nectar into honey? | They add enzymes and fan it with their wings to dry it | They boil it in the hot sun up on the roof of the hive all day | They mix it with water | They chew it slowly into wax
exercise: What do bears eat when they raid a bee nest? | Honey and the bee grubs | Only the wax | Only the honey, never anything else | The adult bees only
exercise: Why does a honeybee die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting sticks in thick skin and is left behind | The person's blood is a strong poison to the bee and kills it | It runs out of breath | It is too sad
exercise: What should you do with a sting left in your skin? | Scrape it out quickly | Leave it in for a day | Push it in deeper | Pour honey on it
exercise: What is a swarm? | The old queen and about half the workers looking for a new home | A gang of angry bees that has decided to go out and attack people | Bees going to sleep | Drones fighting
exercise: How does a swarm choose its new home? | Scouts dance for places and the one with most supporters wins | The queen picks a place at random | The biggest drone decides for everyone | They always go back to the old hive they left
exercise: Why can a bumblebee fly? | It beats its wings very fast and makes lifting swirls of air | Its round body is filled with a light gas, like a party balloon | It is pushed by the wind | It cannot, it only hops
exercise: Why are honeycomb cells six-sided? | Hexagons hold the most honey for the least wax | Bees use their six legs to measure each side | Round cells are too hard to see in the dark hive | Bees cannot make other shapes
exercise: How do guard bees check who comes in? | They smell each bee | They check its colour | They ask it to dance | They count its six legs
exercise: How can a female larva become a queen? | If workers feed it only royal jelly | If it hatches first | If it is a male | If it eats only honey
test: Winnie the Pooh sticks his paw in a hive for honey. What would a real bear be after too? | The juicy bee grubs | The wooden hive | The flowers | Nothing, bears never touch hives
test: «science says bumblebees can't fly, it's a miracle» What is the truth? | That old claim came from a wrong simple calculation | It is true, they float along on a kind of magic nobody can explain | They are pushed by other bees | They fly only downhill, never up
test: A big ball of bees hangs on a branch in the garden. What is the best thing to do? | Leave it and call a beekeeper; swarms are usually calm | Hit it with a stick | Spray it with water | Climb up and look inside to find the queen bee in the middle
test: «bee stung me and the sting's still in, gross» What should he do? | Scrape it out quickly, as it keeps pumping venom | Leave it, it will fly away | Push it in deeper | Squeeze it hard between two fingers for a minute
test: What would happen if a hive had no flowers to visit all summer? | It would have no honey to eat in winter | It would make its honey from rain water | The bees would hibernate in summer | Nothing, bees eat leaves
test: «Nan says the bees are all dying, can I do anything» What helps? | Leave wild flowers or grow lavender | Kill all the wasps | Buy more honey from the shop | Keep a bee in a jar
test: A worker bee waggles straight up on the comb. Which way are the flowers? | Towards the sun | Away from the sun | Straight down into the ground | Wherever the wind blows
test: Count them: 4 bees on a flower. How many legs and how many wings? | 24 legs and 16 wings | 16 legs and 8 wings | 24 legs and 8 wings | 32 legs and 16 wings
test: «the fat one in the middle is the boss and gives orders» What is really true? | The queen lays the eggs; she does not give orders | The queen tells each bee its job every morning | The queen is a male | The queen fetches all the food
test: A bee from another hive lands at the door carrying nectar. What may the guards do? | Sometimes let her in, as if she paid a toll | Always kill her at once | Make her their new queen | Give her all of their honey to take home
exam: Identify the information conveyed by the waggle dance of the honeybee. | The direction and distance of a source of food | The age of the queen | The approach of inclement weather | The number of drones in the hive
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the death of a honeybee subsequent to stinging a human being? | The barbed sting becomes lodged in the skin and is torn from the bee | The venom of the bee is fatal to the bee itself | The bee dies of fright | The bee is incapable of withdrawing its legs from the skin of the person stung
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which honey is produced and stored by honeybees. | It constitutes the food of the colony during winter | It serves as the material of the comb | It is employed in the repulsion of bears | It is produced solely for human consumption
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hexagonal form of the cells of the honeycomb. | It permits the greatest storage of honey for the least expenditure of wax | It is determined by the six legs of the bee | It arises from the cells being made of honey | It serves no purpose whatsoever
